tive":true,"id":486012,"name":"Winjama"},{"active":true,"id":1363616,"name":"Mighty Multitudes"}],"members":null,"namevariations":["Ainsworth Levy","B Levy","B. A. Levy","B. Kelly","B. Levey","B. Levi","B. Levy","B.Levi","B.Levy","B/Levy","Barington Levy","Barrigton Levy","Barringto Levy","Barrington","Barrington - N.H. Levy","Barrington / Levy","Barrington Ainsworth Levy","Barrington L","Barrington Leavy","Barrington Lev","Barrington Levey","Barrington Levi","Barrington Ley","Barrington Lezy","Barrington N.H. Levy","Barrington-N.H. Levy","Barrington/N.H. Levy","Barrinton L.","Barrinton Levy","Barry Levy","Bileny","Brrington Levy","Levi","Levi Barrington","Levy","P. Levy","Sammy Levy"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/25283-Barrington-Levy","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.barringtonlevy.com/","http://www.facebook.com/pages/Barrington-LEVY/467154043417061","http://twitter.com/MrLevy411","http://instagram.com/barringtonlevy411","http://myspace.com/barringtonlevy","http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrington_Levy","http://www.whosampled.com/Barrington-Levy/","http://soundcloud.com/barringtonlevy","http://www.youtube.com/user/BarringtonLevyMusic"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/388836/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"388836","image":"https://i.discogs.com/hWCXvnrJxusWDRetzBhKCmXYFYsgInOPRPTSOwelokA/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:384/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTM4ODgz/Ni0xNzE3NTYzOTU0/LTE4ODguanBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/cHYiDsqyrANUckezn4Eu8N7sW1oew6EDlTP0k1IMTho/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTM4ODgz/Ni0xNzE3NTYzOTU0/LTE4ODguanBlZw.jpeg","profile":"Billy Boyo, born on September 21st, 1969 in Kingston, was a teenage star of pre-digital dancehall. In 1981, while just 12 years old, he was discovered by the legendary producer [a=Henry \"Junjo\" Lawes] and also became part of his Volcano Soundsystem. When Billy Boyo was discovered by Junjo he was dating Billy's sister, Rosemarie Rowe. Junjo recorded an album with him and another young deejay, [a=Little Harry], entitled \"DJ Clash Volume 2\" and released through [l=Greensleeves Records]. Little John and Billy Boyo were both interviewed in the book \"Reggae Inna Dancehall Style\". In 1983, Boyo went to London and recorded another album for the [l=Silver Camel] label, but it didn't get released until 2003, when it saw the light as \"Zim Zim\" on the [l=Silver Kamel Audio] re-issue imprint. Billy Boyo later moved to Brooklyn.\r\n\r\nBilly Boyo was more a live deejay rather than a recording artist. After the digital era set in, it became quiet around him, and he never achieved any kind of comeback. On October 29th, 2000, he apparently died of a brain tumor. This is the most widely accepted theory for Billy Boyo's death, however no records of this can be found.\r\n\r\nBilly Boyo is mentioned in Simple Simon's \"Murderer\" produced by Courtney Cole in 1994. \r\n\r\nBilly Boyo is mentioned in Eek-A-Mouse's \"Gunshot A Cry\" but is called \"Billy Bowe\" rather than \"Billy Boyo\". This song is Eek-A-Mouse's version of \"Big Iron\" by Marty Robbins.\r\n\r\nBilly Boyo is featured on Lee Hi-Power in 1983.\r\n\r\nBilly Boyo has a very short interview in the BBC documentary \"Musical Roots\" about the group Musical Youth.\r\n\r\nBilly Boyo has some footage in the documentary \"Jools In Jamaica\" filmed in 1983.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:388836","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":1896799,"name":"Billy Rowe (2)"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["B. Boyo","Billi Boyo","Billy Bayo","Billy Boy","Billy Boy Ho","Billy Boy O","Billy Boyd","Billy Rowe","Billy. B","Boyd"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/388836-Billy-Boyo","type":"artist","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Billy_Boyo"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/4742/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"4742","image":"https://i.discogs.com/8hsImGSXwjh_2BOpFti2wj56eRzQu0KISryAjTvWK6A/rs:fit/g:sm/q: